## FAQ

**Do I really need a gooseneck kettle, or can I use my regular one?**
You can technically brew pour-over with any kettle, but a regular spout dumps water too fast and too wide to control the pour, which causes uneven extraction and blown brew times. The gooseneck is the single most noticeable pour-over upgrade after the grinder and dripper. If you only make French press, drip-machine, or cold brew, you genuinely do not need one.

**Is a variable-temperature electric kettle worth the extra money over a stovetop?**
For most people, yes — holding water at a precise 200°F without a separate thermometer removes a real variable from your morning. But a stovetop kettle with a built-in thermometer (like the Barista Warrior) closes most of that gap for less money, if you do not mind a minute of attention.

**What temperature should I brew pour-over at?**
Aim for 195–205°F (90–96°C). Lighter roasts like the hotter end of that range; darker roasts do better a few degrees cooler to avoid bitterness. If you only have a stovetop kettle with no thermometer, boil the water and let it rest 30–45 seconds before pouring.

**Why does the pour speed of the spout matter?**
A slower, thinner stream gives you more control over where the water lands and how long the coffee stays submerged, which directly affects extraction. Faster-flowing goosenecks (like the wider Hario spout) are still fine, but they demand a steadier hand — worth knowing if you are a beginner choosing between models.

**Can I use these kettles for tea too?**
Absolutely. The variable-temperature models (COSORI, Bonavita, OXO) are arguably better tea kettles than coffee kettles, since green and white teas want cooler water than coffee. The gooseneck spout is a non-issue for tea — just a bonus-controlled pour.

**Are cheaper no-name gooseneck kettles on Amazon worth it?**
Some are fine, but the two things that fail on ultra-cheap kettles are the temperature electronics and the lid hinge, and warranty support is often nonexistent. The picks above are the ones with enough of a track record that I would trust them with daily use. Verify current pricing and reviews before buying, as budget kettle models get revised and rebranded constantly.
